Knowledge base source content. Ingest with:

  npm run kb:ingest -- ./knowledge

═══ WHAT IS ACCEPTED ═══

  .md  .markdown  .mdx   treated as Markdown
  .txt                   treated as plain text

Everything else is ignored, silently. Subdirectories are walked. Dotfiles are
skipped, which is why this file is not itself indexed. Files must be UTF-8.

NOT yet supported: PDF, DOCX, website URLs, FAQ imports, database sources.
The schema has room for all of them (DocumentSourceType) but no parser exists
yet — that is slice 6b.

A document's title is its path relative to this directory, so
`policies/refunds.md` is titled "policies/refunds.md" and that string is what
appears in the citation panel. Name files the way you want them cited.

═══ HOW TO STRUCTURE IT ═══

These are not style preferences. Each one changes what the assistant retrieves.

1. USE `#` AND `##` HEADINGS, AND MAKE THEM TOPICS.
   A heading starts a new chunk, and its text is stored with that chunk and
   shown in citations. Sections are the strongest signal about what belongs
   together. Underline-style headings (===, ---) are NOT detected — only `#`.

2. ONE TOPIC PER SECTION.
   Retrieval usually returns a single passage. An answer spread across two
   sections may only half-arrive.

3. WRITE EACH SECTION SO IT STANDS ALONE.
   The model sees the retrieved passage, not the document around it. "It is 50
   pesos per hour" is useless out of context; "Parking is 50 pesos per hour
   after the first two hours" is not. Repeating the subject is good here, even
   where it reads as redundant in the full document.

4. SEPARATE PARAGRAPHS WITH BLANK LINES.
   Blank lines are the chunk boundary. A wall of text with no blank lines gets
   split on sentence boundaries instead, which is worse.

5. KEEP SECTIONS ROUGHLY 100-1200 CHARACTERS.
   Above ~1800 a section is split. Very short passages are merged into the
   preceding section rather than dropped, so a one-line fact is fine — it just
   rides along with its neighbours.

6. WRITE IN ENGLISH.
   Visitors speak Taglish and Bisaya; the assistant translates their question
   into English keywords before searching. The embedding model is multilingual
   so a Tagalog document will still work, but English is the tested path.

7. STATE FACTS PLAINLY. NO MARKETING.
   "Open 8am to 5pm, Monday to Friday" retrieves. "We're here when you need
   us!" does not, and the assistant is forbidden from inventing the hours.

═══ TWO THINGS TO KNOW ═══

Anything indexed here is readable in bulk through /api/knowledge/search, which
is public and unauthenticated until slice 7. Do not index anything that should
not be public.

Whatever is not in here, the assistant will say it does not know. That is the
designed behaviour, not a bug — it will not fill gaps from its own knowledge.

Ingestion is idempotent on a SHA-256 of file contents: unchanged files are
skipped, changed files have their chunks replaced wholesale. Re-run it freely.
